Keys are pointers
description: every key is a path portion — a bare integer selects a position, ': x' a name
- >
  Every key is a pointer: the same text that names an entry (`key: value`) is a path portion a pointer
  can travel (`*key`). The `*` sigil dereferences — beyond YAML's bare alias word it takes a full
  **pointer expression**, resolved purely structurally: a bare `*a` is the sibling key `a`, and scopes
  reach wider (the [pointers part](::language:pointers)).
- !!yo
  feline: *pets: 1                   # a sibling's second element
  manager: *: pets: 1                # : = current document root
  remote: *::: pet.store.com: pets   # ::: = the world — never fetched
- >
  Positions are integer keys and names are string keys, told apart by the **bare-token typing rule**:
  a bare integer portion selects the integer key n (`: pets: 1`), a name — or any quoted portion —
  selects the string key (`: '1'` is the numeric string). The old worry of an integer key `1:` versus
  a string key `'1':` dissolves — it is simply `: 1` versus `: '1'`. The retired bracket index `[n]`
  reads forever as an alias, written never; the surviving brackets are the non-literal position
  operators (`&path[]`, `[?]`, `[.±k]`).
